er at its source. It gets picked up on the way to the tap \u2014 from the service line under the street, from solder in older joints, from the brass in the tap itself. That makes it a property of your building more than of your town.","effect":"Infants and children who drink water containing lead in excess of the action level could experience delays in their physical or mental development. Children could show slight deficits in attention span and learning abilities. Adults who drink this water over many years could develop kidney problems or high blood pressure.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"Children and infants first.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Lead"},"Total Haloacetic Acids (HAA5)":{"plain":"Like trihalomethanes, haloacetic acids form when disinfectant reacts with organic matter in the water. They are a side-effect of disinfection, not an added ingredient.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing haloacetic acids in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years may have an increased risk of getting cancer.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Haloacetic Acids","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Total Haloacetic Acids (HAA5)"}}}